<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish Golden Age</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th–17th centuries</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Siglo de Oro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish Golden Age, or Siglo de Oro, was a period of flourishing in Spanish literature and art in the 16th and 17th centuries, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> kings. It produced the novelist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-de-cervantes">Miguel de Cervantes</a>, the playwrights <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega">Lope de Vega</a> and Calderón de la Barca, the poets Garcilaso de la Vega, Góngora and Quevedo, and painters such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-greco">El Greco</a>, Velázquez, Zurbarán and Murillo. It is usually said to end with the death of Calderón in 1681.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-golden-age.jpg" alt="Spanish Golden Age" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zvonimir Stamenov · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monasterio_de_El_Escorial_en_Madrid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
